obably fly  quite as well because we seem to see  that in every hotel room but I think  that I think that what he was doing was  he was resonating with this same idea  that have been there in the  Enlightenment thinkers that that you  have a core of the brain which is  primitive which in his mind would have  been the it'd and then beyond that you  have a ego which is essentially the way  in which the it'd and the brain itself  meld together because that's much more  related to the conscious day-to-day  functioning of course in his mind the  it'd was pre conscious or unconscious  but I don't think it's quite the same  way as we think of pre conscious  activity today but if you put those two  things together  then the self the super-ego is self  command that's something which again  Smith wrote a lot about in his Theory of  Moral Sentiments he recognized that the  child learns self command through  basically growing up to the point where  he realizes he's not the center of the  universe and if you read Freud's  theories about infantile sexuality etc  that is exactly what he's describing  only in slightly different language so I  think there's compatibility with  psychoanalytic theory and the way in  which we now see normal development  thank you  several of the questions this evening  have to do with the violence the  unspeakable violence over the past week  the  incredible acts of cruelty one does not  even want to call them terrorism they  were just outright murder of innocent  civilians people who clearly were  outside of the main social fabric of  their community who really committed  these horrific acts how how do the the  theories of your book and the the  concept of social fabric and and how and  or lack thereof relate to some of the  violence that we see so rampant in in  this country with guns or most recently  in Europe with the the horrible murders  in Paris I have to confess I haven't  thought deeply about that regarding the  Paris tragedy but I think that