 to us we've got more and more  regulations because we are not  regulating ourselves and so I think that  that when you've allowed the the the  thing to run amok so we've got so many  guns in this country we've got more guns  than we have people as I understand it  then we are in fact in a set of  circumstances where we we need  individual trust and caring in you know  there's there's the fascinating thing to  me is that apparently Canada has almost  as many guns per capita as we do but  apparently they have some sort of social  fabric which enables them not to shoot  each other quite so frequently so I  don't know what that all means but we  need to start thinking about it standing  back from the moral issue I deserve to  have a gun because you know it's in the  Constitution or in the  as opposed to the idea that these things  are extremely dangerous I don't know  that we can do very much about the the  situation in Paris except to recognize  that it's going to be a series of things  like that that we have to cope with  because it's a totally different vision  of what society is and we don't share  that Society so we have to oppose it and  I'm not sure how that will play itself  out but I think it's one of the  extraordinary challenges of our time  that we need to think about that as a  free society and how we can possibly  contain something that is so easily  perpetrated upon us now so thank you  thank you great answer to a difficult  question  one thing you you spoke about was if you  really want to understand your your  brother's suffering on the rack you need  to envision yourself on the rack you  need to put yourself in his place and  and one of the questions here I'm not  sure that I fully understand but I'll  put it out there is what do we do about  the deficit of empathy in this country  and I think that some of that relates  really to the mission of the friends in  terms of D stigmatizing mental illness  that is it seems that people have a  remarkable lack