 but it's  something to do with that social link  which makes you remember them  and that's what a child does when they  when they're looking for the social  links in their world they the first of  all draw upon those they have from their  parents and their siblings and then they  imagine them that's why they play these  little games you know with teddys coming  to tea and and Teddy's always spills his  teacup you know this is a big problem  with teddy  and and and and so that imagination that  he had that they have is the beginning  of empathic understanding of the fact  that teddy is a klutz  or and and so these I think that we need  again to nurture that and we can do that  by just being engaged with children when  you then get to to a situation and I've  gone and forgotten the second part of  your question the the the the way in  which as they grow up but an empathy for  those of mental disorders with with  those who who are sick if they if they  have engaged the idea of trying to  understand why you know aren't Mabel who  comes every Thanksgiving and gives them  something very nice but compare it with  uncle Charlie who comes and is always a  grouch  it's that sort of understanding which  then enables them to say why is that  person strange why is it that that  person seems to be distant from me then  they begin to if their parents are  thoughtful they can begin to understand  empathically what happens we don't do  nearly enough of that I mean we I joke  with my friends you know we walk past  people in the street yes who are  obviously seriously mentally ill you see  them in local restaurants I've had I've  had people come up to me in local  restaurants who have serious illness and  I but but the fact is that we don't  engage them as if they're real people I  remember a patient I had in Vermont once  she came in tied to a chair she had  mania tied to a chair by her family  because they didn't know whatever to do  with her she was completely crazed so  they were