y the time you get to  UCLA you could still learn some things  and I think one of the things that Jane  particularly has tried to Jane someone  has particularly tried to do is to is to  foster that idea of course we do good  science of course we know we are  pursuing the vulnerabilities that people  that create mental illness you're not  going to solve problems by this alone we  need to know how developmental disorders  begin we need to know how degeneration  occurs in the central nervous system we  know all those things and once we know  all those things we can then find ways  in which we can improve them but in but  in general we also need to maintain a  sense of what it is that the human  person can do I'm I think human beings  are extraordinary creatures and I think  we we have extraordinary gifts and I  think that we as I said at the very  beginning of the talk we have left we  have lost some of our best attributes in  the last few years we as we've been  washed over by increasing frenzy and  Technic technologies if we could if we  could change that here in America we  would be actually providing something  which was written in the early  declaration we didn't like King George  because he was a he was from the top  down we liked from the bottom up and we  have to be careful we don't slip into  just being a top down Society again and  so I think we can do that at a  university we can say if people remember  what they learned here when they become  CEOs or or whatever they are leaders  they will be also caring of the  individuals who they work with  great thank you wonderful answer  in just a moment we're going to close  the program and invite you to join dr. Y  Brown in the foyer where you can  purchase and get him to sign your book  but first I want to thank you very much  dr. Bob Rafferty has a wonderful  presentation and and a lively discussion  of C I think very challenging questions  that you raised in your book and thank  you very much for participating in